 When Russell explains his philosophy as a “ takeaway-er ” (a person who takes from the takers) — and then promptly extracts cash from a food truck, only to hand some of it to two little girls — the sequence is less a plot point than a demonstration of style. The man is not trying to hide. He is trying to see how far the surface will hold.
 Taylor Sheridan has written every episode, and the trailer does not pretend this is a radical departure from the Tulsa King formula . It is a relocation of that formula into a more comically constrained landscape, with Jackson’s particular gravity doing the work that Stallone’s physical presence did in the parent series.
 The young supporting characters (a college dropout, a hotel valet, and a pair of cheerleaders) are sketched rather than revealed, but the point is not ensemble depth. The point is the sight of Jackson, in a flat cap and round glasses, treating suburban Texas as just another territory that has not yet been properly explained to him.
 Trailers are advertisements, and this one advertises pleasure. Whether the series can sustain that pleasure remains to be seen.
 'Frisco King's Trailer Proves the Location Shift Was Worth It
 Paramount+ 
 Paramount 
 Close Paramount+ 
 Paramount 
 There was a moment, not long after Samuel L. Jackson first appeared opposite Sylvester Stallone in Tulsa King , when the spinoff was called NOLA King and pointed back toward New Orleans. The idea was tidy: Jackson’s Russell Lee Washington Jr., having spared Dwight Manfredi’s life because of an old prison debt, would return home and try to build something of his own.
 Dave Erickson ( Sons of Anarchy, Mayor of Kingstown ) was set to run it, but things didn't play out as planned. Erickson soon stepped away amid the demands of other Sheridan shows, and the project changed hands and cities.
 What arrived instead is Frisco King , set in a North Texas suburb. The relocation is not merely logistical, though Sheridan’s production base already sits nearby. New Orleans already possesses its own criminal mythology, so a man arriving there to start over would be stepping into a story the city had already told. Frisco, by contrast, is newer, more polished, and less familiar for Russell.
