 Moreover, no one could be better suited to the role than Samuel L. Jackson. For decades, he has turned threat into conversation. He has a blunt delivery, delivers lines with sudden precision, and can make a character who has done terrible things sound like someone explaining the weather. Russell, a hitman who once shared a prison cell with Sylvester Stallone's Dwight Manfredi and chose not to finish the job, needs exactly that quality.
 Jackson does not have to raise his voice to make these rarely explored suburbs feel important. He simply looks at a minivan and a lemonade stand as if they are the latest problems he has already solved. And Sheridan, with his extensive TV experience, is the right person to continue bringing the best out of the legendary actor.
